[[en:Kimon]] [[ja:鬼門]] [[en:Kimon (鬼門)]] [[sn:Kimon]]
<!--Kimon-->
==Merged second translation== {{otheruses||{{ill|Dir En Grey|ja|DIR EN GREY|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Dir En Grey|qq=}}'s {{ill|Music Video|ja|ミュージック・ビデオ|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Music Video|qq=}}|鬼門 (映像作品)}} '''Kimon''' (鬼門) refers to the northeastern direction ({{ill|Ushitora|ja|艮|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ushitora|qq=}}, "Ox and Tiger": between the {{ill|Ox|ja|丑|1=Ox (zodiac)|lt=Ox (丑)|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ox|13=User:Immanuelle/Ox (zodiac)|qq=}} and {{ill|Tiger|ja|寅|1=Tiger (zodiac)|lt=Tiger (寅)|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tiger|13=User:Immanuelle/Tiger (zodiac)|qq=}} on the Chinese zodiac). In Japan, this direction has traditionally been considered ominous and is associated with the entrance and exit of demons.
== Overview ==
The direction opposite to Kimon, the southwest ({{ill|Kon|ja|坤|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kon|qq=}}, {{ill|Hinoeuma|ja|未申|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hinoeuma|qq=}}), is called '''Urakimon''' (裏鬼門) and is similarly regarded as inauspicious. Kimon (northeast) is associated with "demon gate," while the southwest (Urakimon) is referred to as "people gate" or "illness gate," the southeast ({{ill|Sun|ja|巽|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sun|qq=}}) as "wind gate," and the northwest as "heaven gate" according to {{ill|Onmyōdō|ja|陰陽道|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Onmyōdō|qq=}}.<ref>{{Cite book|title=日本人なら知っておきたい正しい家相の本|author=小池康寿|publisher=プレジデント社|year=2015|page=37}}</ref> In the {{ill|Konjaku Monogatari|ja|今昔物語集|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Konjaku Monogatari|qq=}}, an {{ill|Onmyōji|ja|陰陽師|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Onmyōji|qq=}} named {{ill|Yuge no Koreo|ja|弓削是雄|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yuge no Koreo|qq=}} predicts an assassin's presence in the northeast direction of {{ill|Tomonokimi Yotsugi|ja|伴宿禰世継|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tomonokimi Yotsugi|qq=}}’s residence, illustrating a perception of艮as an unlucky direction.<ref>{{Cite book|author=村山修一|title=日本陰陽道史話|publisher=平凡社|year=1988|page=101|ISBN=4-582-76406-1}}</ref> In {{ill|Taiwan|ja|台湾|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Taiwan|qq=}}, Kimon is considered the "gateway between this world and the next," opening during the {{ill|Ghost Month|ja|鬼月|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ghost Month|qq=}} in the seventh lunar month.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://jp.taiwantoday.tw/news.php?unit=190&post=103467|title=「鬼月」と呼ばれるLunisolar Julyの意味|work=TAIWAN TODAY|date=2016-08-12}}</ref>
== Reasons for Fearing Kimon ==
The term "Kimon" appears in the Eastern Han period's {{ill|Wang Chong|ja|王充|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Wang Chong|qq=}}’s ''{{ill|Lunheng|ja|論衡|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Lunheng|qq=}}'', in the ''Correcting Ghosts'' chapter, citing the ''{{ill|Classic of Mountains and Seas|ja|山海経|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Classic of Mountains and Seas|qq=}}''.<ref>{{Cite book|author=水野杏紀|title=易、風水、暦、養生、処世 東アジアの宇宙観|publisher=講談社選書メチエ|year=2016|pages=28-29}}</ref>
{{Quotation|According to the ''Classic of Mountains and Seas,'' there is a Dousaku Mountain (度朔山) in the Eastern Sea. Atop the mountain stands a giant peach tree that spans 3,000 ri, curving around its branches. The area between the branches to the northeast is called Kimon, where myriad ghosts pass through. Two divine figures guard this place, binding malevolent ghosts with reed ropes to be fed to tigers. Consequently, the Yellow Emperor established rituals, erecting a large peachwood doll at the gate and hanging reed ropes to ward off evil spirits.|}}
In ancient China, the transition from winter to spring marked the year’s end, a period associated with ghosts causing diseases and calamities. Rituals were performed to drive out these spirits and welcome spring’s fortune. These practices, transmitted to Japan, evolved into seasonal events like {{ill|Setsubun|ja|節分|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Setsubun|qq=}}.<ref>{{Cite book|author=水野杏紀|title=易、風水、暦、養生、処世 東アジアの宇宙観|publisher=講談社選書メチエ|year=2016|pages=31-32}}</ref>
Kimon's association with demons also stems from its connection to {{ill|Onmyōdō|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Onmyōdō|qq=}} practices that integrated with Japan's {{ill|Shinbutsu-shūgō|ja|神仏習合|1=Shinbutsu-shūgō|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinbutsu-shūgō|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinbutsu-shūgō|qq=}}.<ref>{{Cite book|author=小池康寿|title=日本人なら知っておきたい正しい家相の本|publisher=プレジデント社|year=2015|pages=36-37}}</ref>
== Perceptions of Kimon (Authority) ==
Rulers established rituals like the Kyoto’s {{ill|Keijō Shishū Ekijin Sai|ja|京城四隅疫神祭|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Keijō Shishū Ekijin Sai|qq=}} to protect the city from disasters.<ref>{{Cite book|author=小池康寿|title=日本人なら知っておきたい正しい家相の本|publisher=プレジデント社|year=2015|page=37}}</ref> For example, the monk {{ill|Jien|ja|慈円|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Jien|qq=}} described {{ill|Mount Hiei|ja|比叡山|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Mount Hiei|qq=}} as guarding Kyoto’s northeastern Kimon direction.<ref>{{Cite book|author=村山修一|title=日本陰陽道史話|publisher=平凡社|year=1988|page=128}}</ref>
== Popular Practices ==
Traditionally, objects such as {{ill|holly|ja|ヒイラギ|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/holly|qq=}} and {{ill|Nandina|ja|ナンテン|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nandina|qq=}} were planted in northeast corners to repel misfortune. Kyoto’s {{ill|Gosho|ja|京都御所|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Gosho|qq=}} features a recessed northeastern section known as {{ill|Sarugakuzuka|ja|猿ヶ辻|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sarugakuzuka|qq=}}, believed to protect against Kimon.<ref>{{Cite book|author=小池康寿|title=日本人なら知っておきたい正しい家相の本|publisher=プレジデント社|year=2015|page=28}}</ref>
