[[sn:Ura Kimon]] [[en:Ura Kimon]] [[ja:鬼門]]
<!--Ura Kimon--><!--Ura Kimon--><!--Ura Kimon--><!--Ura Kimon--><!--Ura Kimon--><!--Ura Kimon--><!--Ura Kimon--><!--Ura Kimon-->
[[Category:Merged pages]]

'''Kimon''' (鬼門) refers to the {{ill|northeast|ja|北東|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/northeast|qq=}} direction ({{ill|艮|1=Gen (Bagua)|2=ja|3=艮|lt=Gen (艮)|ja|艮|1=Gen (Bagua)|2=ja|3=艮|lt=Gen (艮)|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/艮|qq=}}, representing the space between {{ill|Ox (zodiac)|ja|丑|1=Ox (Zodiac)|lt=Ox (丑)|2=ja|3=丑|1=Ox (zodiac)|lt=Ox (丑)|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ox (zodiac)|13=User:Immanuelle/Ox (zodiac)|qq=}} and {{ill|Tiger (zodiac)|ja|寅|1=Tiger (Zodiac)|lt=Tiger (寅)|2=ja|3=寅|1=Tiger (zodiac)|lt=Tiger (寅)|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tiger (zodiac)|13=User:Immanuelle/Tiger (zodiac)|qq=}}), considered an inauspicious direction in Japanese tradition, believed to be the gateway for malevolent spirits.
== Overview == The direction opposite to {{ill|Kimon|ja|鬼門|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kimon|qq=}}, the {{ill|southwest|ja|南西|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/southwest|qq=}} ({{ill|Kun|ja|坤|1=Kun (Bagua)|2=ja|3=坤|lt=Kun (坤)|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kun|qq=}}, representing {{ill|Goat (zodiac)|ja|未|1=Goat (Zodiac)|lt=Goat (未)|2=ja|3=未|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Goat (zodiac)|qq=}} and {{ill|Monkey (zodiac)|ja|申|1=Monkey (Zodiac)|lt=Monkey (申)|2=ja|3=申|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Monkey (zodiac)|qq=}}), is known as the '''Ura-Kimon''' (裏鬼門) and is similarly regarded with apprehension. In {{ill|Onmyōdō|ja|陰陽道|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Onmyōdō|qq=}}, the four corners are designated as gates: {{ill|Kimon|ja|鬼門|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kimon|qq=}} in the northeast, {{ill|Fūmon|ja|風門|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fūmon|qq=}} or {{ill|Chimon|ja|地門|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Chimon|qq=}} in the southeast, {{ill|Jinmon|ja|人門|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Jinmon|qq=}} or {{ill|Byōmon|ja|病門|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Byōmon|qq=}} in the southwest, and {{ill|Tenmon (direction)|ja|天門|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tenmon (direction)|qq=}} in the northwest.
== Reasons for Fear of Kimon == The term "{{ill|Kimon|ja|鬼門|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kimon|qq=}}" appears in ancient Chinese texts, such as the {{ill|Lunheng|ja|論衡|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Lunheng|qq=}} by {{ill|Wang Chong|ja|王充|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Wang Chong|qq=}}, referencing the {{ill|Shan Hai Jing|ja|山海経|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shan Hai Jing|qq=}}. It describes a large peach tree guarding the gate through which countless spirits pass. Although this passage isn't present in current versions of "Shan Hai Jing," it illustrates early beliefs associating the northeast with spirits.
== Interpretations by Rulers == In historical Japan, rulers performed rituals at the four corners of the capital to prevent misfortune. The {{ill|Heian period|ja|平安時代|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Heian period|qq=}} saw the rise of fear toward {{ill|Kimon|ja|鬼門|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kimon|qq=}}, believed to cause calamities like disease, disasters, and curses. For example, {{ill|Mount Hiei|ja|比叡山|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Mount Hiei|qq=}} was considered to block the "demon gate" of {{ill|Kyoto|ja|京都|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kyoto|qq=}}.
== Popular Beliefs == Common folk also practiced protective measures against {{ill|Kimon|ja|鬼門|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kimon|qq=}}. In {{ill|Kyoto Imperial Palace|ja|京都御所|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kyoto Imperial Palace|qq=}}, a carved monkey ({{ill|Saru|ja|猿|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Saru|qq=}}) guards the northeast corner, known as {{ill|Sarugatsuji|ja|猿ヶ辻|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sarugatsuji|qq=}}. People planted {{ill|holly|ja|ヒイラギ|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/holly|qq=}} and {{ill|nandina|ja|ナンテン|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/nandina|qq=}} near their homes' northeastern corners to ward off evil.
== Denial of Kimon Beliefs == During the {{ill|Meiji Restoration|ja|明治維新|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Meiji Restoration|qq=}}, modernization labeled {{ill|Kimon|ja|鬼門|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kimon|qq=}} beliefs as superstition. Scholars like {{ill|Arinori Mori|ja|森有礼|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Arinori Mori|qq=}} criticized such practices, though traditional beliefs persisted among the public.
== References == * Seikei Kiyoshi, ''Modern House Fortunes'' (1989), ISBN 978-4106019678 * Yasuhisa Koike, ''The Correct Home Fortunes for Japanese People'' (2015), ISBN 978-4833421492
